{"name":"Seoul","slug":"seoul","region":"East Asia","lat":37.57,"lon":126.98,"seasons":{"DJF":{"season_name":"Dec 2026–Feb 2027","years":[1951,1952,1953,1954,1955,1956,1957,1958,1959,1960,1961,1962,1963,1964,1965,1966,1967,1968,1969,1970,1971,1972,1973,1974,1975,1976,1977,1978,1979,1980,1981,1982,1983,1984,1985,1986,1987,1988,1989,1990,1991,1992,1993,1994,1995,1996,1997,1998,1999,2000,2001,2002,2003,2004,2005,2006,2007,2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025],"roni_lead":[-0.38,0.98,0.04,0.84,-0.31,-1.31,-0.14,1.16,0.24,0.04,0.08,-0.16,-0.3,1.06,-0.64,1.86,-0.03,-0.08,0.56,0.67,-0.57,-0.4,1.82,-1.63,-0.45,-0.95,1.04,0.89,-0.08,0.52,-0.06,-0.17,2.03,-0.9,-0.61,-0.18,1.0,1.2,-1.71,-0.23,0.27,1.2,0.1,0.46,0.97,-0.93,-0.25,2.22,-1.33,-1.06,-0.55,-0.18,1.18,0.17,0.7,-0.18,0.73,-1.33,-0.38,0.86,-1.61,-0.91,0.21,-0.22,0.35,2.03,-0.96,-0.82,0.62,0.15,-1.37,-1.08,-1.1,1.3,-0.76],"paragraph":"Dec–Feb in Seoul has warmed by about 0.25 °C per decade since 1951, roughly 1.7 °C in all, with the coldest days warming fastest, so cold extremes are becoming rarer. There is a clear El Niño signal on temperature: after El Niño conditions in the preceding fall, Dec–Feb tends to run warmer. With this year's strong El Niño, that push is about 30% of a normal year-to-year swing, a noticeable tilt, though ordinary variability can easily outweigh it, roughly as much warmth as 18 years of the long-term trend. Dec–Feb rainfall shows no clear long-term trend, but El Niño has a clear effect toward a wetter season with more rainy days, and this year's shift is substantial. Humidity shows no clear long-term trend, but El Niño has a clear effect toward a more humid Dec–Feb, and this year's shift is substantial. For Dec 2026–Feb 2027, a warmer-than-normal season is more likely than not (about 55%), about 0.9 °C above the 1991–2020 average, mostly because of the long-term warming trend rather than El Niño; a wetter-than-normal season is more likely than not (about 65%), and a more humid season than normal is more likely than not (about 55%). The El Niño expected for Sep–Nov 2026 is stronger than any in the record used here, so the outlook extends the historical relationship beyond what has been observed. This outlook is based on the El Niño strength expected for Sep–Nov 2026 and will be updated when it is observed in early December.","variables":{"temperature":{"normal_1991_2020":-1.2164,"trend_per_decade":0.2329,"trend_p":0.0023,"trend_total_since_1951":1.7469,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.4174,"enso_p":0.018,"confidence":"Clear","survives_fdr":false,"fraction_explained":0.0753,"intrinsic_sd":1.3708,"effect_this_season":0.4303,"effect_ratio":0.3139,"size":"Noticeable","size_typical_el_nino":"Negligible","once_every_n_years_without_el_nino":null,"expected_anomaly_2027":0.8695,"forecast_mean":-0.3469,"forecast_sd":1.4413,"p_below":0.1842,"p_near":0.2608,"p_above":0.5549,"forecast_words":"More likely than not (55%): a warmer-than-normal season","within_record":false,"enso_effect_shrunk_per_degC":0.1701,"shrink_factor":0.4075,"effect_this_season_raw":1.0559,"trend_part_2027":0.4392,"roni_upper_within_record":false,"years_of_warming_equivalent":18.4743,"percentiles":{"p05":{"trend_per_decade":0.4052,"trend_p":0.0054,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.362,"enso_p":0.2877,"confidence":"No identifiable effect","shift_this_season":0.9159,"lines_cross":false},"p50":{"trend_per_decade":0.1926,"trend_p":0.0027,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.5054,"enso_p":0.0016,"confidence":"Strong","shift_this_season":1.2788,"lines_cross":false},"p95":{"trend_per_decade":0.1456,"trend_p":0.1295,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.3493,"enso_p":0.0333,"confidence":"Clear","shift_this_season":0.8838,"lines_cross":false},"tails":{"enso_difference":-0.0127,"enso_difference_p":0.9729,"trend_difference":-0.2597,"trend_difference_p":0.0897}},"values":[-3.365,-1.382,-3.551,-0.289,-3.152,-2.559,-4.603,-3.351,0.41,-0.379,-3.053,-1.444,-4.33,-1.289,-2.184,-2.293,-3.321,-4.304,-1.5